"""Build the study narrative and supplement from audited portable results."""
from pathlib import Path
import html,json
HOME=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
a=json.loads((HOME/'data/analysis.json').read_text());lat=json.loads((HOME/'data/latency-summary.json').read_text())
STYLE='''body{max-width:920px;margin:42px auto;padding:0 24px;color:#17212a;background:white;font:18px/1.65 Georgia,serif}h1{font-size:34px;line-height:1.2;text-align:center}h2{font-size:25px;margin-top:2em}h3{font-size:20px}a{color:#145e84}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font:14px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif}td,th{text-align:left;padding:9px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid #d4dce1}th{border-top:2px solid #667680}figure{margin:28px 0}img,video{max-width:100%;height:auto}video{width:320px;image-rendering:pixelated}figcaption,.note{font:14px/1.55 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#465662}.status{padding:12px 16px;background:#f1f5f7;font:14px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif}.meta{text-align:center;font:14px system-ui,sans-serif;color:#576570}.scroll{overflow-x:auto}.flow{display:flex;align-items:stretch;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;font:15px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif}.flow .box{flex:1;min-width:150px;padding:14px;border:1px solid #b9c9d2;background:#f5f8fa;border-radius:5px}.flow b{display:block;color:#155c7d}.arrow{align-self:center;font-size:24px}.game{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:24px;flex-wrap:wrap}.game img{width:320px;image-rendering:pixelated}.game div{flex:1;min-width:220px}code{font-size:.85em}li{margin:.4em 0}@media print{body{margin:0;max-width:none;font-size:11pt}.status,video{display:none}h2{break-after:avoid}figure,table{break-inside:avoid}}'''
def page(title,body):return f'<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{title}</title><style>{STYLE}</style></head><body>{body}</body></html>'
def table(headers,rows):
 return '<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</th>' for x in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+str(v)+'</td>' for v in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'
def results(env,models):
 rows=[]
 for model in models:
  m=a['cohorts'][f'{env}|{model}|6'];lo,hi=m['wilson95']
  rows.append([model,f"{m['wins']}/{m['n']}",f"{100*m['rate']:.0f}%",f'{lo*100:.1f}% to {hi*100:.1f}%',f"{m['mean_episode_median_latency_ms']:.0f}"])
 return table(['Policy','Wins','Win rate','Nominal 95% interval','Latency, ms'],rows)
body='''<p class="status">Technical article · 19 October 2026 · <a href="#methods">Methods</a> · <a href="supplement.html">Supplement</a> · <a href="reproduction.html">Code and reproduction status</a></p>
<h1>Teaching a small language model to play</h1>
<p class="meta">Behavior cloning → reinforcement learning in Kittie Has Other Plans</p>
<p>I trained a 0.8-billion-parameter language model to play a Game Boy boss encounter. It won 39 of 50 games after learning from demonstrations and 47 of 50 after reinforcement learning. Here is the resulting policy in action.</p>
<figure id="video"><video playsinline controls preload="metadata" poster="images/game-frame.png" src="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/videos/ppo15-greedy/common-win/gameplay.mp4"></video><figcaption>PPO15 playing Head Goon. This winning replay was selected after evaluation and reconstructed from recorded native actions. Kittie is on the left; Head Goon is on the right. <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/ppo10-vs-ppo15-diversity.html">Additional comparisons and loss recordings</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="story">Learn from demonstrations, then play for rewards</h2>
<p>In <em>Kittie Has Other Plans</em>, Kittie must hit Head Goon three times with a wool ball while the boss is vulnerable. That means moving, jumping, timing throws and recovering the ball. The game keeps running while the model decides what to do.</p>
<p>I started with pretrained Qwen3.5-0.8B. Behavior cloning teaches it to imitate successful gameplay demonstrations from JEV, a separate model that also serves as an evaluation reference. That produces BC04. I then use proximal policy optimization, or PPO, to train the policy on its own Head Goon episodes, rewarding completion and boss hits and penalizing damage and failure.</p>
<figure><div class="flow"><div class="box"><b>Pretrained Qwen</b>0.8B parameters</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box"><b>Behavior cloning</b>Successful JEV demonstrations<br>Result: BC04</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box"><b>Head Goon PPO</b>16 iterations<br>Reported: PPO15</div></div><figcaption>The training sequence is behavior cloning followed by PPO. PPO specializes the behavior-cloned policy to Head Goon.</figcaption></figure>
<p>The policy reads a text description of the current game state and chooses button controls. The video shows what happened in the game; the model receives positions, health and other state information. <a href="#methods">The methods below</a> specify the observation, action choices and training setup.</p>
<p>I wanted to see how much PPO improved the trained encounter, then whether that performance held up when I changed the boss's starting position and attack schedule.</p>
<h2 id="results">The trained encounter: 78% to 94%</h2>

<p>These evaluations use the original encounter: the same saved starting state and boss schedule used for PPO training. Each policy plays 50 games. Qwen chooses its highest-probability action at each decision.</p>
'''+results('main',(['Vanilla Qwen'] if 'main|Vanilla Qwen|6' in a['cohorts'] else [])+['JEV','BC04','PPO15'])+'''
<p class="note">Latency is the mean of episode-median observation-to-action times, excluding the post-action interval. This statistic is used consistently in both main tables.</p>
<p>PPO15 won 47/50 games, compared with 39/50 for BC04 and 26/50 for JEV. Relative to BC04, the observed improvement is 16 percentage points, with nominal 95% interval 2.2 to 29.8 points and Fisher p = 0.0407. Relative to JEV, it is 42 points, with interval 25.3 to 56.1 points and p = 2.65 × 10<sup>−6</sup>. PPO15 performs better in these recorded evaluations. Differences in serving conditions and action selection limit what I can attribute to training alone.</p>

<p>I chose PPO15 as the headline checkpoint after seeing evaluation results. The comparison is exploratory, and these repeated games measure performance from one fixed start.</p>
<h2 id="variation">Changing the encounter exposes a limit</h2>
<p>Next I tested 50 shared scenarios with different boss positions and attack schedules, without further training. The advantage was less clear.</p>
<figure><img src="performance.png" alt="Win rates and nominal 95 percent intervals for JEV, BC04 and PPO15 in original and randomized encounters"><figcaption>All three policies are evaluated on the original encounter and the same randomized scenario set. Bars show nominal 95% Wilson intervals. Separate serving runs and action-selection procedures limit causal interpretation.</figcaption></figure>
'''+results('randomized',['JEV','BC04','PPO15'])+'''
<p>Observed win rates fall to 32% for BC04 and 50% for PPO15. PPO15 wins 15 scenarios that BC04 loses; BC04 wins 6 that PPO15 loses. The paired difference is 18 points, with nominal bootstrap interval 0 to 36 and exact p = 0.0784. The data favor PPO15 numerically but leave improvement under scenario variation uncertain.</p>
<p>JEV wins 60% of scenarios and has lower response latency. Its ten-point advantage over PPO15 has paired interval −6 to 26 points and p = 0.3593. This does not establish a win-rate difference or equivalence. These evaluations do not isolate why the observed rates differ. All outcomes remain included.</p>
<h2 id="limits">What I take from this</h2>
<p>The clearest result is strong performance on the encounter used for PPO training. The policy wins less often when the encounter changes. PPO15 still wins more randomized scenarios than BC04, but this sample leaves the size of that improvement uncertain.</p>
<p>JEV and Qwen differ in training history, action selection and deployment latency. JEV's model size is not established here. The <a href="supplement.html#latency">delay sensitivity experiment</a> approximately matches PPO15's central latency distribution and substantially reduces JEV's observed win rate, but retains a longer slow-response tail. It supports sensitivity to deployment timing without establishing that latency explains the entire cross-model difference.</p>
<p>The study contains one training lineage, selection informed by evaluation outcomes, and adaptive follow-up. Original encounters repeat a fixed entry. Original Qwen evaluations used A10 hardware, while randomized PPO evaluations used A10G; fixed-probe checks found one changed top action among eight PPO15 observations across those workers. Thus the entire original-to-randomized difference cannot be attributed solely to scenario variation. Independent retraining and controlled serving conditions are needed for stronger algorithmic conclusions.</p>
<h2 id="methods">How I trained and evaluated the policy</h2>
<h3>Game and policy interface</h3>
<p>The player must hit Head Goon three times with a wool ball while the boss is vulnerable, then reach the encounter's completion state. The player can move, jump, throw the ball along a flat or lobbed path, and recover it after a throw. The boss alternates between movement, attacks and vulnerability windows. Contact can damage the player. I call the fixed starting state and standard boss schedule used for PPO training the <em>original encounter</em>.</p>
<figure class="game"><img src="images/game-frame.png" alt="Recorded Head Goon encounter: Kittie on the left, Head Goon on the right"><div><b>Head Goon encounter</b><p>Kittie is on the left; Head Goon is on the right. The player must time throws to hit the boss during vulnerability windows.</p><p class="note">Frame extracted at 2 seconds from the retained PPO15 winning replay. <a href="#video">Watch the replay</a>.</p></div><figcaption>The evaluated game. The policies receive textual state information, not this rendered image.</figcaption></figure>
<p>At each decision, the emulator supplies a structured text observation containing positions, health, held buttons, boss phase, ball state, recent events and derived physical forecasts. Forecasts use information available at the observation time; they are not observations of the future. An <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/data/jev-head-goon-variation-001/prompt-example.json">actual randomized-encounter prompt</a> shows the state fields and mechanics instructions.</p>
<figure><div class="flow"><div class="box"><b>Observe</b>Current game state and recent history</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box"><b>Compute</b>Text observation → action probabilities</div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div class="box"><b>Apply</b>Movement, A-button and throw controls</div></div><figcaption>One request is processed at a time. During computation the game advances with the previous buttons held. After application, the game advances six frames before the next observation.</figcaption></figure>
<p>Each action combines the following controls, giving 3 × 2 × 3 = 18 possibilities:</p>
'''+table(['Component','Choices'],[['Horizontal control','Neutral, left, right'],['A-button control','Released, held'],['Throw control','None, flat, lob']])+'''
<p>For example, “right + A held + lob” holds Right, A, B and Up together. Jumping and throwing require fresh button presses, so holding A or B does not repeatedly trigger the action. In dedicated Qwen evaluations, I choose the combined action with the highest probability. JEV returns a movement/A-button choice and a throw choice for each facing direction; I apply the throw choice matching the resulting direction. Neutral movement preserves the current facing. Both use the same physical controls, but their selection procedures differ. The <a href="supplement.html#actions">supplement lists all 18 combinations and the probability calculation</a>.</p>
<h3>Supervised and reinforcement fine-tuning</h3>
<p>Behavior cloning starts from pretrained Qwen3.5-0.8B and uses successful JEV demonstrations from five game levels. The resulting checkpoint is BC04. The initialization and supervised-stage details appear in <a href="supplement.html#bc">Appendix A</a>. JEV's evaluation games are separate from these success-filtered training demonstrations. Whole demonstration trajectories remain in their assigned training, validation or held-out splits.</p>

'''+table(['Setting','Behavior cloning','Head Goon PPO'],[
['Training data','Successful JEV demonstrations from five levels','Fresh complete Head Goon episodes'],
['Schedule','Supervised stages specified in Appendix A','16 iterations, up to 2 passes each'],
['Actor learning rate','5 × 10<sup>−5</sup>, then 10<sup>−5</sup>','3 × 10<sup>−6</sup>'],
['Effective batch size','32','64, accumulated singleton forwards'],
['Adapter / optimizer','Rank-8 LoRA / AdamW','Same adapter / continuous PPO state'],
['Compute configuration','One Modal A10 worker at a time','One Modal A10 worker at a time']])+'''
<p>The final BC stage uses 70 successful training trajectories with 4,423 decisions, including 552 Head Goon decisions from 11 trajectories. Validation and held-out splits contain 1,360 and 1,632 decisions. BC minimizes weighted negative log likelihood. <a href="supplement.html#bc">Appendix A</a> specifies the full BC sequence, weighting and source records.</p>
<p>Each PPO iteration collects at least 512 decisions and eight complete episodes, allowing whole-episode overshoot. Rollouts sample actions at temperature 1. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347">PPO</a> uses clipping coefficient 0.2, entropy coefficient 0.01 and numerical/KL safeguards. Recorded action probabilities, values and advantage targets remain fixed during each update. A separate critic predicts remaining discounted reward from detached Qwen features; critic fitting does not update Qwen. Actor, critic, optimizer and random-number-generator state continue across iterations. Full settings appear in <a href="supplement.html#training">Appendix B</a>.</p>
<p>The reward encourages completion and boss damage, penalizes injury, failure and elapsed time, and favors hits with fewer preceding rebounds:</p>
'''+table(['Event','Reward'],[
['Complete encounter','+100; additionally +2 per remaining heart and +2 per carried ball'],
['Damaging boss hit','+20 × 0.5<sup>b</sup>, where b is the number of preceding surface rebounds in that throw'],
['Unique boss defeat','+2'],['Player damage','−4 per heart lost'],['Player death','−30'],['Episode time limit','−5'],['Elapsed game time','−0.1 per second']])+'''
<p class="note">Pickup and a new throw reset the rebound count. Boss-hit response, resting contact and the top of a flight arc do not count as surface rebounds. Traversal-only distance and bell rewards are inactive in Head Goon. <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/head_goon_ppo/rewards.py">Boss reward implementation</a> and <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/rl/rewards.py">shared event rewards</a>.</p>
<h3>Evaluation and scenario variation</h3>
<p>I completed 16 PPO iterations and chose PPO15 as the primary reported checkpoint using subsequent 50-game evaluations. These are exploratory comparisons rather than independent tests of a checkpoint chosen in advance. PPO10 remains a secondary analysis in the supplement.</p>
<p>The original-encounter cohorts contain 50 games per policy from one saved starting state. The randomized cohorts use 50 shared scenarios generated independently of their outcomes. I vary initial boss position uniformly over integer x coordinates 256 through 304, patrol duration uniformly over 27 through 81 updates at each patrol, and the number of hops uniformly over one, two or three at each sequence. The vulnerability window remains 72 updates and other mechanics are unchanged. These parameters were not randomized during training.</p>
<p>All policies receive current countdowns and committed hop counts; future schedule draws remain hidden. Each scenario supplies the same native entry and schedule to every policy. Each game ends at completion, death, 90 seconds or 5,400 frames. Qwen uses greedy joint actions; JEV uses its returned choices. Model inference occurs while the game runs. <a href="supplement.html#latency">Appendix C</a> reports measured timing distributions and the separate JEV delay experiment; <a href="supplement.html#interval">Appendix D</a> reports the one-frame sensitivity check.</p>
<h3>Analysis</h3>
<p>I report nominal 95% Wilson intervals for win rates. Separate original-encounter cohorts use two-sided Fisher tests and unpaired score intervals. Randomized comparisons use whole-scenario pairing, bootstrap intervals for the difference and exact two-sided McNemar tests. Analyses are exploratory, with no multiplicity correction. Fixed-entry repetitions can share trajectories and timing correlations; intervals do not include uncertainty across independent training runs.</p>
<h2 id="code">Code and reproduction</h2>
<p>The companion code centers on the training sequence, behavior cloning followed by PPO. The <a href="reproduction.html">reproduction guide</a> describes the extracted training code and the remaining work to connect model loading, game assets and native evaluation. The package also regenerates statistics and figures from 510 exported evaluation records, with scenario and delay schedules supplied as inputs. Full standalone training and evaluation packaging remains incomplete. The <a href="supplement.html#evidence">supplement links all detailed reports</a>.</p>
'''
baseline=a['cohorts'].get('main|Vanilla Qwen|6')
if baseline:
 baseline_note=f"<p>The untouched Qwen model won {baseline['wins']}/{baseline['n']} games before any gameplay fine-tuning. The outcomes were two deaths and 48 timeouts, with no boss hits. Its nominal 95% win-rate interval is {baseline['wilson95'][0]:.1%} to {baseline['wilson95'][1]:.1%}; zero observed wins does not establish a zero underlying success probability. I used the same textual observations and greedy action-scoring interface as BC04 and PPO15, with no adapter or prompt search. This measures the pretrained model under that interface, not its best achievable gameplay with different prompting. <a href='https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/head-goon-qwen-base-001-results.html'>Full vanilla-Qwen results</a>.</p>"
 body=body.replace('It won 39 of 50 games after learning from demonstrations and 47 of 50 after reinforcement learning.',f"The untouched model won {baseline['wins']} of {baseline['n']} games under the evaluation interface. After learning from demonstrations it won 39 of 50, and after reinforcement learning it won 47 of 50.")
 body=body.replace('All three policies are evaluated on the original encounter and the same randomized scenario set.','Vanilla Qwen is evaluated on the original encounter only. JEV, BC04 and PPO15 are also evaluated on the same randomized scenario set.')
 body=body.replace('510 exported evaluation records','560 exported evaluation records')
 body=body.replace('The clearest result is strong performance on the encounter used for PPO training.', 'The pretrained model failed to win under this action interface, while BC04 won 39/50 and PPO15 won 47/50. This makes learning from demonstrations the first substantial gain in the recorded comparison. The clearest result after PPO is strong performance on the encounter used for training.')
 body=body.replace('Win rates and nominal 95 percent intervals for JEV, BC04 and PPO15 in original and randomized encounters','Win rates and nominal 95 percent intervals, including vanilla Qwen on the original encounter')
else:
 baseline_note='<p class="status">Vanilla-Qwen baseline, provisional: the latest confirmed result is 0/49, with the final game and audit pending. The working expectation is 0/50. This assumption is not included in the statistical tables or figures. The baseline uses the same observations and greedy action-scoring interface as BC04 and PPO15, without an adapter or prompt search.</p>'
body=body.replace('<p class="note">Latency is the mean',baseline_note+'\n<p class="note">Latency is the mean',1)
(HOME/'index.html').write_text(page('Teaching a small language model to play',body))
# Supplement retains methodological details and exploratory results outside the main narrative.
sup='''<p class="status"><a href="index.html">Return to the article</a> · <a href="reproduction.html">Reproduction package</a></p><h1>Supplementary methods and analyses</h1>
<h2 id="bc">A. Behavior-cloning lineage</h2>
<p>Training starts from the official pretrained Qwen3.5-0.8B weights with a fresh rank-eight LoRA adapter. Five Courtyard BC epochs precede four multi-level BC epochs. The latter starts from the former's saved adapter with a fresh optimizer. “BC04” names the fourth multi-level epoch, not the fourth supervised epoch overall.</p>
'''+table(['Stage','Distinct training decisions','Epochs','Example presentations','Optimizer updates','Learning rate'],[['Courtyard BC',779,5,'3,895',125,'5 × 10<sup>−5</sup>'],['Multi-level BC','4,423',4,'17,692',556,'10<sup>−5</sup>']])+'''
<p>Courtyard BC accounts for about 18% of the combined example presentations and 18% of the optimizer updates. Its learning rate is five times higher. Its Courtyard validation cross-entropy fell from 4.005 to 0.519. These observations establish that it was a substantive fitting stage; they do not measure its contribution to later Head Goon performance. No ablation omits this stage, so I do not assume its effect was smaller than the later stage's.</p>
<p>The multi-level stage weights trajectories equally within each level and assigns level mass proportional to the square root of its number of training trajectories. Training, validation and held-out splits have 4,423, 1,360 and 1,632 decisions from 70, 26 and 21 trajectories. Recorded request-time delay estimates stay attached to their demonstration labels. <a href="data/training-lineage.json">Counts and source hashes</a> · <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/bc-main-results.html">Courtyard BC report</a> · <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/bc-multi-005-results.html">BC04 report</a>.</p>
<h2 id="training">B. Policy, critic and optimization</h2>
<p>The base revision and checkpoint hashes are recorded in <a href="study.json">study.json</a>. Training uses AdamW, rank-eight LoRA and gradient norm clipping at 1. The configured Modal worker has one A10 GPU, four CPU cores and 24 GiB host memory. Inference and PPO forward passes are singleton; gradient accumulation gives an effective PPO batch of 64.</p>
'''+table(['PPO setting','Value'],[['Actor / critic learning rate','3 × 10<sup>−6</sup> / 10<sup>−3</sup>'],['Collection minimum','512 decisions and 8 complete episodes per iteration'],['PPO passes / clip / entropy coefficient','2 / 0.2 / 0.01'],['KL target / global stop / per-level stop','0.02 / 0.03 / 0.06'],['Discount per frame / GAE decay per 20 frames','0.9997 / 0.99'],['Critic network','LayerNorm → Linear 1024→128 → tanh → Linear 128→1'],['Critic fitting','Huber loss; up to 200 epochs; whole-episode validation every 25; restore best fit and optimizer']])+'''
<p>The critic input is a detached 1,024-dimensional hidden representation from Qwen; 1,024 is the loaded model's hidden width. Its output is a scalar estimate of discounted remaining reward. Fixed generalized-advantage targets combine realized rewards and request-time value estimates. Initial critic fitting uses retained BC04 Head Goon episodes before the first PPO rollout. Continuation preserves actor, critic, optimizer and RNG state. The actor's optimizer reaches step 288 after iteration 16 and step 270 at PPO15.</p>
<p>Historical runs included sampled monitoring evaluations. These supplied no PPO or critic targets and did not change the training state used for continuation. The researcher chose the reported checkpoint using later dedicated evaluations. Original training records retain all monitoring outcomes and state provenance.</p>
<h3 id="actions">B.1. The 18 physical actions</h3>
<p>Every row below is combined with each of the three throw columns. The action-list index runs from 0 through 17 in row-major order. Equal joint probabilities are resolved by the earliest entry.</p>
'''+table(['Movement / A control','No throw','Flat throw','Lob throw'],[[m,*[f'{i*3+j}: {m} + {t}' for j,t in enumerate(['none','flat','lob'])]] for i,m in enumerate(['Neutral / released','Neutral / held','Left / released','Left / held','Right / released','Right / held'])])+'''
<p>Qwen's readout supplies six movement/A probabilities and two three-way throw distributions, one for each facing direction. For a candidate action, its joint probability is the movement probability multiplied by the throw probability for the resulting facing. Greedy evaluation maximizes that product over all 18 actions. JEV uses its returned movement choice and the corresponding returned throw choice. These choices are evaluated from the same observation; the description does not imply two sequential API calls. Controls are held until replaced; jump and throw events require fresh button presses. <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/bc_multi/contract.py">Observation and action contract</a>.</p>
<h2 id="latency">C. JEV with an empirical latency floor</h2>
<p>This exploratory experiment asks how JEV performs when most responses are delayed to approximate PPO15's serving latency. A fixed schedule independently samples from all 2,399 recorded PPO15 applied-action delays. Early JEV responses wait until their assigned application frame; late responses are applied when ready. The native game advances throughout. Future targets are hidden from the policy. The schedule is supplied in <a href="data/timing-input.json">timing-input.json</a>; reproduction need not reconstruct the historical delay pool.</p>
<figure><img src="latency-distributions.png" alt="Empirical cumulative latency distributions, with a separate view of the slowest five percent"><figcaption>Figure S1. Actual observation-to-action latency for applied responses. Each response contributes one observation; longer episodes contribute more responses. The left panel shows the central distribution and the right panel expands its upper tail. All observations, including outliers, are retained in the exported data.</figcaption></figure>
'''+table(['Policy','Applied responses','Mean ms','Median ms','95th percentile ms','99th percentile ms','Maximum ms'],[[name,lat[name]['n'],*[f'{lat[name][k]:.1f}' for k in ['mean','median','p95','p99','max']]] for name in ['Original JEV','PPO15','Delayed JEV']])+'''
<p>PPO15 and delayed JEV have nearly identical median and 95th-percentile latencies, with differences below 1.1 ms. The mean differs by 10.9 ms, or 3.5%. This is a close empirical match for those central summary measures, not equality of the entire distributions. Delayed JEV's 99th percentile is 532.4 ms versus PPO15's 352.3 ms. Delayed JEV has 15 of 2,128 responses above one second; PPO15 has none of 2,399. Original JEV already has a slow tail, with 19 of 3,015 responses above one second.</p>
<p>In total, 168 of 2,128 delayed-JEV applied replies exceed their assigned target frame, or 7.9%. The independent schedule does not recreate relationships between prompt state, prompt length and latency, or correlations across successive requests. Terminal replies that never affected the game are excluded from this applied-response distribution and retained in the original audit. These limits prevent treating the experiment as exact timing parity.</p>
<p>Original JEV wins 30/50 scenarios and delayed JEV wins 14/50. Original-only wins number 21 and delayed-only wins number 5. The original-minus-delayed difference is 32 percentage points, with nominal paired 95% bootstrap interval 14 to 50 and exact McNemar p = 0.00249. This is evidence of sensitivity to the delay intervention in these runs. It does not isolate a universal latency effect or explain the entire JEV–PPO difference.</p>
<p><a href="data/latencies.json">Applied-response data and source hashes</a> · <a href="data/latency-summary.json">Distribution summaries</a> · <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/jev-head-goon-latency-001-results.html">Full audited delay report</a>. The main tables use means of episode medians; this appendix pools responses to describe the full distribution.</p>
<h2 id="interval">D. One-frame sensitivity check</h2>
<p>In a separate exploratory check, PPO15 advances one frame after action application before the next observation. Its timing instructions change accordingly. It wins 15/30 original-encounter games and 19/30 randomized scenarios. Historical six-frame results on the same 30 randomized scenarios are 17/30. The paired difference is 6.7 percentage points, with nominal interval −16.7 to 30 and exact p = 0.7905.</p>
<p>The original-encounter result differs substantially from the historical 47/50 six-frame wins. However, workers and timing instructions also differ. The experiment establishes sensitivity to the changed deployment protocol, without isolating the frame interval as the cause. It is not used to choose a new training protocol. <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/head-goon-ppo15-oneframe-002-combined.html">All one-frame outcomes and evidence</a>.</p>
<h2>E. Secondary checkpoint and behavioral analyses</h2>
<h3>Original encounter</h3>'''+results('main',['PPO10','PPO15'])+'<h3>Randomized encounters</h3>'+results('randomized',['PPO10','PPO15'])+'''
<p>PPO10 wins all 50 original games, but the difference from PPO15 is uncertain, Fisher p = 0.2424. Under variation, PPO15's 14-point advantage over PPO10 has paired interval −4 to 32 and p = 0.2100. Neither result establishes a superior checkpoint across both settings.</p>
<p>On the original encounter, BC04, PPO10 and PPO15 produce 8, 17 and 9 distinct applied action sequences in 50 games each. The largest repeated sequence occurs in 25, 17 and 25 games, respectively. Thus BC04 is already repetitive by this measure. On randomized scenarios each policy produces 50 distinct sequences, which can result from the changed scenarios without indicating 50 distinct strategies. <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/reports/bc04-ppo-diversity/index.html">Coverage, stalls, entropy and sequence metrics</a> · <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/ppo10-vs-ppo15-diversity.html">Comparison videos</a>.</p>
<h2 id="evidence">F. Evidence and scope</h2>
<p>All completed outcomes in the reported cohorts remain in the analysis. No new gameplay, model inference or API calls were used to prepare this revision. Timing and training-stage analyses use retained records. Checkpoint selection and adaptive extensions remain disclosed; nominal tests have no multiplicity correction.</p><ul><li><a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/jev-head-goon-baseline-001-results.html">JEV original encounter</a></li><li><a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/head-goon-bc04-greedy-001-results.html">BC04 original encounter</a> and <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/head-goon-bc04-variation-001-results.html">randomized encounters</a></li><li><a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/head-goon-greedy-010015-001-combined.html">PPO original encounter</a> and <a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/head-goon-variation-010015-001-results.html">randomized encounters</a></li><li><a href="https://jintais-macbook-air.tail6a9202.ts.net/kittie-rl/jev-head-goon-variation-001-results.html">JEV randomized encounters</a></li><li><a href="data/analysis.json">Portable statistical results</a> and <a href="data/sources.json">export source hashes</a></li></ul>'''
